# The two vendored prompt assets are byte-pinned, not merely text.
#
# `src/core/prompt.ts` compares each file's SHA-256 against a constant compiled
# into it and throws at import when they differ, because the instruction and the
# chat template have to reach the model exactly as the fine-tune was trained on
# them. A paraphrase is a different task, and so is a CRLF.
#
# That makes git's line-ending conversion a correctness bug rather than a
# cosmetic one. On a Windows machine with `core.autocrlf=true` — which is the
# default on GitHub's windows runners and on most Windows installs — a plain
# `git clone` rewrites every LF in these files to CRLF. The bytes change, the
# hash no longer matches, and the module throws on import. CI caught this: on
# windows-latest every test that transitively imports `dist/core/prompt.js`
# failed, while the same commit was green on ubuntu and macos.
#
# `-text` (not `text eol=lf`) is deliberate. It tells git these are binary and
# must never be converted, so what lands in the working tree is exactly the blob
# that was committed — the invariant the hash pin actually needs. `eol=lf` would
# also produce LF here, but it normalises the working tree toward a line ending
# git chose rather than leaving the bytes alone, and it would silently rewrite
# the file at checkout if a CRLF version were ever committed instead of letting
# the hash check fail loudly.
assets/chat_template.jinja -text
assets/instruction.txt -text
